+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: `plist`
+
+## 1. Summary
+
+Added 13 hegel property-based tests to the `plist` crate (Apple property list
+reading/writing): 8 round-trip/precision properties in `src/value.rs`
+(`mod hegel_tests`) and 5 untrusted-input robustness properties plus 2
+known-bug pins in `tests/fuzzer.rs` (`mod hegel_robustness`). Setup was a
+single `cargo add --dev hegeltest`; `.hegel/` was added to `.gitignore`.
+
+Two real bugs were found and pinned:
+
+1. **Panic** re-serializing far-future dates as XML (untrusted-input reachable).
+2. **Process abort** (stack overflow) when dropping a `Value` parsed from a
+   small, deeply nested XML input.
+
+Everything else — including the classic binary-plist attack surface of
+corrupted trailers, inflated object counts, and out-of-range offsets — held up
+under default and 10x-case-count runs. The crate's existing fuzzing
+(`fuzz/fuzz_targets` for the ascii/binary/xml readers, plus the regression
+corpus in `tests/fuzzer.rs`) clearly hardened the *decode* path; the bugs found
+were in the gaps the fuzz targets do not cover: re-serialization of decoded
+values and the lifecycle (drop) of decoded values.
+
+## 2. Properties Added
+
+`src/value.rs` — `mod hegel_tests` (round-trips over generated `Value` trees):
+
+| Test | Property |
+|---|---|
+| `xml_roundtrip_recovers_value` | `to_writer_xml` → `from_reader_xml` recovers an equal `Value`, and the round-trip is a byte-level fixpoint (re-serializing yields identical XML). |
+| `binary_roundtrip_recovers_value` | `to_writer_binary` → `from_reader` is bit-exact (including NaN payloads and `Uid`s), and a fixpoint at the byte level. |
+| `xml_and_binary_encodings_agree` | The same `Value` written as XML and as binary reads back to the same value from both encodings. |
+| `integer_boundary_values_roundtrip_both_formats` | `i64::MIN/MAX`, `u64::MAX`, `i64::MAX as u64 + 1`, 0, ±1 and full `i64`/`u64` ranges round-trip exactly through both formats. |
+| `real_boundary_values_roundtrip_both_formats` | Every `f64` bit pattern (drawn via `from_bits`: subnormals, ±0.0, ±∞, NaN payloads) round-trips bit-exactly through binary, and through XML up to NaN canonicalization. |
+| `date_with_nanoseconds_roundtrips_xml_exactly` | Nanosecond-precision dates round-trip exactly through XML (RFC 3339 carries 9 subsecond digits). |
+| `date_binary_roundtrip_within_f64_precision` | Binary dates (stored as f64 seconds since 2001) round-trip within 2 ULPs of that representation + 1ns. |
+| `string_edge_codepoints_roundtrip_both_formats` | Strings built from targeted codepoints (ASCII/C1 controls, `\r`, NEL, LS/PS, non-characters U+FFFE/U+FFFF, supplementary planes) round-trip through both formats — targeting the XML escape-set boundary and the binary ASCII/UTF-16 representation split. |
+
+`tests/fuzzer.rs` — `mod hegel_robustness` (untrusted-decoder robustness):
+
+| Test | Property |
+|---|---|
+| `arbitrary_bytes_decode_never_panics` | `from_reader`/`from_reader_xml`/`from_reader_ascii` on arbitrary bytes (boosted with `bplist00` and XML-prologue prefixes) return `Ok`/`Err`, never panic/hang/OOM. |
+| `corrupted_binary_trailer_never_panics` | A valid binary plist with any trailer field (offset size, ref size, object count, root ref, offset-table offset) overwritten with arbitrary/boundary values is handled gracefully. |
+| `inflated_object_count_is_rejected` | Inflating the trailer's object count (up to `u64::MAX`) is always rejected with `Err` and never causes a huge up-front allocation (validation/rejection converse of the no-panic property). |
+| `corrupted_valid_plist_never_panics` | Bit-flipping or truncating a valid plist (either encoding) never panics. |
+| `deeply_nested_xml_parses_without_stack_overflow` | XML arrays/dicts nested up to 20,000 deep parse `Ok` when well-formed and `Err` when unclosed, without stack overflow (values are dismantled iteratively in the harness — see bug 2). |
+
+Plus the two pinned bug tests described below.
+
+## 3. Generators and Methodology
+
+- **Recursive `Value` generator** (`arb_value`, `compose!` + `BoxedGenerator`
+  per the hegel recursion pattern): all variants — `Boolean`, `Integer`
+  (full `i64`/`u64` plus boosted `MIN`/`MAX`/0/±1/`i64::MAX+1`), `Real`
+  (arbitrary `u64` bit patterns + `floats()` + boosted specials), `String`
+  (full-unicode `text()`), `Data`, `Date`, nested `Array`/`Dictionary`
+  (depth-bounded), and `Uid` behind an `allow_uid` flag because
+  `to_writer_xml` correctly errors on `Uid` (`UidNotSupportedInXmlPlist`).
+- **Strict structural comparison** (`value_eq`): `Real` compared by
+  `to_bits()` (derived `PartialEq` would hide `-0.0`/`0.0` confusion and can
+  never accept NaN), dictionaries compared in iteration order (stronger than
+  `IndexMap`'s order-independent `PartialEq`; both encodings preserve key
+  order). XML comparisons pass `canonical_nan` because the XML encoding
+  serializes every NaN as the string `"NaN"` — payload/sign loss there is
+  format lossiness, verified by probing, not a bug.
+- **Bounded date range** (~years 1019–8942), documented in-code as protecting
+  the tests from bug 1 below (out-of-range dates panic when XML-formatted *or
+  Debug-printed*), not as a library contract.
+- **Trailer corruption by construction**: the binary trailer layout
+  (6 pad + offset size + ref size + 3×u64) was transcribed from
+  `src/stream/binary_reader.rs::read_trailer` and fields are overwritten
+  byte-exactly, with drawn values boosted toward `0`, `file_len`, `u32::MAX`,
+  `u64::MAX`.
+- **Depth drawn directly** for the nesting property (uniform recursive
+  generation never reaches interesting depths), swept to 20,000.
+- The generator is duplicated in slimmed form in `tests/fuzzer.rs` because
+  `#[cfg(test)]` unit-test helpers can't be shared with integration tests.
+
+## 4. Bugs Found (kept failing / ignored — not fixed, per instructions)
+
+1. **`known_failure_far_future_date_xml_write_should_error_not_panic`**
+   (`tests/fuzzer.rs`, deterministic, fails on every run):
+   `Value::Date` outside `time::OffsetDateTime`'s range (beyond year 9999)
+   panics — `"overflow adding duration to date"` inside the `time` crate —
+   when written as XML instead of returning `Err`. `Date::to_xml_format`
+   (src/date.rs:40) unwraps the RFC 3339 format result, and the
+   `SystemTime → OffsetDateTime` conversion panics before even that.
+   **Untrusted-input reachable**: the binary format stores dates as f64
+   seconds, so a *valid* binary plist containing a far-future date is accepted
+   by `Value::from_reader`, and re-serializing (or merely `Debug`-printing,
+   since `Date: Debug` calls `to_xml_format`) that value panics.
+
+2. **`known_failure_deeply_nested_value_drop_overflows_stack`**
+   (`tests/fuzzer.rs`, `#[ignore]`d because it aborts the process with
+   SIGABRT; run with `--ignored` to reproduce): parsing a ~700KB XML input of
+   100,000 nested `<array>`s succeeds (parser and builder are iterative), but
+   *dropping* the resulting `Value` overflows the stack — the derived drop
+   glue recurses once per nesting level. `Clone` and `PartialEq` recurse the
+   same way (they overflow around depth 2,000 in debug builds). A small
+   attacker-supplied document therefore aborts any process that parses it and
+   lets the value go out of scope.
+
+Also surfaced (documented, not a bug): binary date precision degrades with
+distance from the 2001 epoch (~4µs per ULP at 545 years out) — an inherent
+property of the f64-seconds representation, found by the 10x run and encoded
+into `date_binary_roundtrip_within_f64_precision`'s tolerance.
+
+## 5. Verification and Results
+
+- **Liveness**: two assertions were temporarily inverted
+  (`xml_roundtrip_recovers_value`, `inflated_object_count_is_rejected`); both
+  failed with minimal shrunk counterexamples (`Boolean(false)`, `extra = 1`),
+  then were reverted and the `.hegel` failure database cleared.
+- **High-count exploratory run**: all hegel tests were run at
+  `test_cases = 1000` (10x default) with temporary per-test case counters
+  confirming ≥1000 cases actually executed. This run found the date-precision
+  boundary above; after deriving the tolerance from the format's f64
+  precision, all properties pass at 10x. Committed tests use the default count.
+- **Final state**: `cargo test --no-fail-fast` → lib 68/68 pass (incl. the 8
+  new properties), doc-tests and macro tests pass, `tests/fuzzer.rs` 16/16
+  pass with exactly 1 deliberate failure (bug 1 pin) and 1 `#[ignore]`d
+  aborting reproducer (bug 2, verified to SIGABRT when run with `--ignored`).
+- **Not covered**: the ASCII (OpenStep) reader has no writer, so no ASCII
+  round-trip exists; it is exercised only via the arbitrary-bytes robustness
+  property (its dedicated fuzz target remains the deeper instrument). The
+  serde layer (`ser`/`de`) was out of scope.
